= Algorithm =

== Overview ==
An '''algorithm''' is a well-specified procedure for transforming an input or state toward a result. Algorithms are fundamental to [[Computation|computation]] because they describe ''how'' a task is to be carried out independently of any one [[Programming language|programming language]] or machine.

An algorithm may later be expressed as a [[Program (computing)|program]], but the two are not identical. The same algorithm can have many implementations, and a program can contain many algorithms together with input/output handling, interfaces, error handling, and other behavior.

== Definition and scope ==
NIST describes an algorithm as a computable set of steps for achieving a desired result. Classical mathematical accounts usually add that an algorithm has a finite description, follows effective rules, and terminates after finitely many steps when it is intended to produce a completed result.

Modern computing complicates a rigid termination rule. Interactive services, online algorithms, controllers, randomized procedures, and distributed systems may continue operating indefinitely while still containing algorithmic procedures. For that reason, precise claims should state the relevant [[Model|model]] of computation and whether termination, responsiveness, or continuing interaction is being required.

== Algorithm, computation, program, and heuristic ==
These nearby ideas should remain separate.

* An '''algorithm''' is the method or procedure.
* A [[Computation|computation]] is the carrying out of computational operations or state transitions.
* A [[Program (computing)|program]] is an executable or interpretable expression of computational behavior in a particular representation.
* A '''heuristic''' is a practical strategy that may trade completeness, optimality, or formal guarantees for usefulness or efficiency.

The same algorithm can be written in different languages, and two different algorithms can solve the same problem.

== Core properties ==
Useful questions about an algorithm include:

* '''Inputs and outputs''' — what information is supplied and what result or behavior is expected?
* '''Specification''' — what problem or relation is the algorithm intended to realize?
* '''Correctness''' — does the algorithm satisfy that specification?
* '''Termination or progress''' — under what conditions does it finish or continue making useful progress?
* '''[[Computational complexity|Complexity]]''' — how do time, memory, communication, or other resource requirements scale?
* '''[[Data structure|Data structures]]''' — how is information organized for the operations the algorithm performs?
* '''Determinism''' — are transitions fixed by the current state and input, or can randomness or nondeterminism affect execution?
* '''Approximation''' — is an exact result required, or is a bounded/heuristic result acceptable?

== Representation ==
Algorithms can be described in mathematical notation, pseudocode, flowcharts, natural language, control tables, or a programming language. A representation should not be confused with the algorithm itself: representations can differ while preserving the relevant procedure.

== Historical development ==
Algorithmic procedures long predate electronic computers. Arithmetic and geometric procedures were used in ancient mathematics. The modern word derives through Latin forms of the name of the ninth-century mathematician al-Khwarizmi.

In the 1930s, work by Church, Turing, Post, Kleene, and others formalized effective calculability through models such as lambda calculus, recursive functions, and Turing machines. These results clarified which functions are computable, but the mathematical characterization of an ''algorithm itself'' remains a richer question than merely identifying the function it computes. Gurevich's abstract-state-machine program is one influential attempt to characterize sequential algorithms through explicit postulates.

== Correctness and efficiency ==
Correctness and efficiency are different properties. An algorithm can be correct but impractically expensive, or efficient on common inputs without being correct for all inputs.

Correctness is often established by a combination of specification, invariants, proofs, tests, and assumptions about the computational model. Efficiency is commonly analyzed asymptotically, but the relevant resource may be time, memory, communication, storage access, energy, or another constrained quantity.

== Major families ==
Common families include deterministic, randomized, online, parallel, distributed, approximation, numerical, cryptographic, graph, search, sorting, optimization, and learning algorithms. These classifications overlap: one algorithm can belong to several families.

== Relationship to data structures ==
Algorithms and [[Data structure|data structures]] are tightly coupled. A representation can make an operation cheap or expensive, and an algorithm may depend on invariants maintained by a structure. For example, binary search depends on ordered data, while graph algorithms depend strongly on how graph relationships are represented.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Algorithm" is an established concept, but its exact boundary varies between mathematics, theoretical computer science, software practice, and popular usage. In particular, popular discussions often call any automated decision process an "algorithm" even when it is better described as a heuristic, model, policy, or large software system.

